# Linux Commands question bank
@subject Linux Commands | $_ | #7C3AED | Files, permissions, text processing, processes, scripting and networking

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Files & Directories

[EASY]
Q: Which command prints the current working directory?
A) cd
B) pwd
C) ls
D) dir
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command lists files including hidden ones (names starting with a dot)?
A) ls -l
B) ls -a
C) ls -h
D) ls -r
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command creates a new directory called projects?
A) touch projects
B) mkdir projects
C) md projects
D) newdir projects
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What does cd .. do?
A) Goes to the home directory
B) Goes to the parent directory
C) Goes to the root directory
D) Lists the parent directory
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command removes a directory and everything inside it?
A) rmdir dir
B) rm -r dir
C) del dir
D) rm dir
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: rmdir only removes empty directories; rm -r removes recursively (use with care).

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of these commands (run in an empty directory)?
CODE bash
mkdir -p a/b/c
touch a/x.txt a/b/y.txt a/b/c/z.txt
find a -name "*.txt" | wc -l
END
A) 3
B) 1
C) 2
D) 6
ANSWER: A
HINT: mkdir -p creates the whole path; find searches recursively.
EXPLAIN: find lists x.txt, y.txt and z.txt at all depths; wc -l counts the 3 lines.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of these commands (run in an empty directory)?
CODE bash
echo hello > f.txt
cp f.txt g.txt
mv g.txt h.txt
echo world >> h.txt
ls
cat h.txt | wc -l
END
A) f.txt h.txt 2
B) f.txt g.txt h.txt 2
C) h.txt 1
D) f.txt h.txt 1
ANSWER: A
HINT: mv renames; >> appends a line instead of overwriting.
EXPLAIN: g.txt is renamed to h.txt, so only f.txt and h.txt remain; h.txt has two lines.

[MEDIUM]
Q: You are in /home/ravi/projects/app. Which command takes you to /home/ravi/notes?
A) cd ../../notes
B) cd ../notes
C) cd ~/projects/notes
D) cd /notes
ANSWER: A
HINT: Count how many levels you must go up before you can go down into notes.
EXPLAIN: app -> projects (..), projects -> ravi (../..), then into notes.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of these commands (run in an empty directory)?
CODE bash
echo data > orig.txt
ln orig.txt hard.txt
ln -s orig.txt soft.txt
rm orig.txt
cat hard.txt
cat soft.txt 2>/dev/null || echo broken
END
A) data broken
B) broken broken
C) data data
D) broken data
ANSWER: A
HINT: A hard link points to the same inode; a symbolic link points to the file NAME.
EXPLAIN: Deleting orig.txt removes one name, but hard.txt still references the inode. soft.txt points to a name that no longer exists.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of these commands (run in an empty directory)?
CODE bash
touch a.log b.log c.txt
for f in *.log; do mv "$f" "${f%.log}.bak"; done
ls
END
A) a.bak b.bak c.txt
B) a.log.bak b.log.bak c.txt
C) a.bak b.bak c.bak
D) a.log b.log c.txt
ANSWER: A
HINT: ${f%.log} removes the shortest match of .log from the END of $f.
EXPLAIN: Each .log file is renamed to the same name with .bak; c.txt is not matched by *.log.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Permissions & Users

[EASY]
Q: In the permission string -rwxr-xr--, what permissions does the group have?
A) rwx
B) r-x
C) r--
D) ---
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: After the file type character come owner (rwx), group (r-x) and others (r--).

[EASY]
Q: Which command changes the permissions of a file?
A) chown
B) chmod
C) chgrp
D) perm
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What numeric value does the permission r (read) have in octal notation?
A) 1
B) 2
C) 4
D) 7
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: r = 4, w = 2, x = 1.

[EASY]
Q: Which command runs a single command with administrator (root) privileges?
A) su -c only
B) sudo
C) root
D) admin
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command changes the owner of a file?
A) chmod
B) chown
C) usermod
D) passwd
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: After chmod 754 script.sh, what is the permission string?
A) rwxr-xr--
B) rwxrw-r--
C) rwxr-x--x
D) rw-r-xr--
ANSWER: A
HINT: Convert each digit separately: 7 = 4+2+1, 5 = 4+1, 4 = 4.
EXPLAIN: 7 = rwx (owner), 5 = r-x (group), 4 = r-- (others).

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of these commands (run in an empty directory)?
CODE bash
touch f
chmod 640 f
chmod g+w,o+r f
stat -c %a f
END
A) 664
B) 640
C) 674
D) 666
ANSWER: A
HINT: Start from 640 and add write for group and read for others.
EXPLAIN: Group goes from 4 (r) to 6 (rw); others from 0 to 4 (r): 664.

[MEDIUM]
Q: The umask is 022. What permissions does a newly created regular file get?
A) 644 (rw-r--r--)
B) 755 (rwxr-xr-x)
C) 666 (rw-rw-rw-)
D) 600 (rw-------)
ANSWER: A
HINT: New files start from 666 (no execute bit); the umask bits are removed.
EXPLAIN: 666 with group and others write bits (022) masked off gives 644. Directories start from 777 and would get 755.

[HARD]
Q: A directory has permissions drwx--x--x. What can another (non-owner) user do?
A) List the directory contents with ls
B) Access a file inside if they already know its exact name (and the file permits it), but cannot list the directory
C) Create new files in it
D) Nothing at all
ANSWER: B
HINT: For directories, r means "list names", w means "create/delete entries", x means "enter / traverse".
EXPLAIN: With only x, others can traverse into the directory and open known filenames, but without r they cannot list it, and without w they cannot create files.

[HARD]
Q: What special permission does chmod u+s /usr/bin/passwd set, and why is it needed?
A) Sticky bit, so only owners can delete files
B) SetUID, so the program runs with the file owner's (root's) privileges and can update /etc/shadow
C) SetGID, so new files inherit the group
D) It makes the file immutable
ANSWER: B
HINT: The s appears in the OWNER execute position.
EXPLAIN: SetUID makes the process run with the effective UID of the file owner (root), allowing ordinary users to change their own password in the protected shadow file.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Text Processing & Pipes

[EASY]
Q: Which command searches for lines matching a pattern in a file?
A) find
B) grep
C) locate
D) search
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What does the | (pipe) symbol do?
A) Runs two commands at the same time without connecting them
B) Sends the output of the first command as input to the second
C) Writes output to a file
D) Comments out the rest of the line
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command shows the first 10 lines of a file?
A) tail
B) head
C) more
D) first
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What does wc -l file.txt print?
A) The number of words
B) The number of lines
C) The number of characters
D) The longest line
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which redirection appends output to the end of a file instead of overwriting it?
A) >
B) >>
C) <
D) 2>
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this command?
CODE bash
printf "apple\nbanana\napple\ncherry\nbanana\napple\n" | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -1
END
A) 3 apple
B) 2 banana
C) 1 cherry
D) apple 3
ANSWER: A
HINT: uniq -c only merges ADJACENT duplicates, which is why sort comes first.
EXPLAIN: The counts are apple 3, banana 2, cherry 1; sort -rn puts the highest count first.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this command?
CODE bash
printf "id,name,marks\n1,asha,82\n2,ravi,91\n3,kiran,76\n" | awk -F, 'NR>1 && $3>80 {print $2}'
END
A) asha ravi
B) ravi
C) asha ravi kiran
D) name asha ravi
ANSWER: A
HINT: -F, sets the field separator. NR>1 skips the header line.
EXPLAIN: Rows with marks above 80 are asha (82) and ravi (91); awk prints the second field, one per line.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this command?
CODE bash
echo "the cat sat on the mat" | sed 's/at/og/g' | cut -d' ' -f2-4
END
A) cog sog on
B) cat sat on
C) cog sat on
D) the cog sog
ANSWER: A
HINT: The g flag replaces every occurrence on the line. cut -f2-4 keeps fields 2 to 4.
EXPLAIN: sed gives "the cog sog on the mog"; fields 2-4 are "cog sog on".

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this command?
CODE bash
printf "error disk\nwarn cpu\nerror net\ninfo ok\nERROR mem\n" | grep -ic '^error'
END
A) 3
B) 2
C) 4
D) 1
ANSWER: A
HINT: -i ignores case and -c prints the count of matching lines. ^ anchors at line start.
EXPLAIN: "error disk", "error net" and "ERROR mem" match case-insensitively at the start: 3.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of these commands?
CODE bash
printf "b 3\na 1\nb 5\na 4\nc 2\n" | awk '{s[$1]+=$2} END {for (k in s) print k, s[k]}' | sort
END
A) a 5 b 8 c 2
B) a 1 b 3 c 2
C) b 8 a 5 c 2
D) a 4 b 5 c 2
ANSWER: A
HINT: s is an associative array keyed by the first column; values are summed per key.
EXPLAIN: a = 1 + 4 = 5, b = 3 + 5 = 8, c = 2. awk's for-in order is unspecified, so the output is piped through sort.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Processes & System

[EASY]
Q: Which command shows a live, continuously updated view of running processes?
A) ps
B) top
C) jobs
D) proc
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command sends a signal (by default SIGTERM) to a process ID?
A) stop
B) kill
C) end
D) exit
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What does adding & at the end of a command do?
A) Runs it with root privileges
B) Runs it in the background
C) Runs it twice
D) Saves its output
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command shows disk space usage of mounted file systems?
A) du
B) df
C) free
D) lsblk only
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: df shows free/used space per file system; du shows the size of directories and files.

[EASY]
Q: Which command shows how much RAM is used and free?
A) free -h
B) df -h
C) mem
D) ram
ANSWER: A

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the difference between kill -15 PID and kill -9 PID?
A) -15 (SIGTERM) asks the process to exit cleanly and can be handled; -9 (SIGKILL) cannot be caught and stops it immediately
B) -9 is polite and -15 is forced
C) They are identical
D) -15 pauses the process; -9 resumes it
ANSWER: A
HINT: Which signal can a program catch to save its work before exiting?
EXPLAIN: SIGTERM lets the program clean up (close files, etc.); SIGKILL is handled by the kernel and cannot be ignored.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this command?
CODE bash
sleep 0.2 & wait $!; echo "exit=$?"
END
A) exit=0
B) exit=1
C) exit=$?
D) Nothing
ANSWER: A
HINT: $! is the PID of the last background job; wait returns that job's exit status.
EXPLAIN: sleep finishes successfully, so wait returns 0 and $? is 0.

[MEDIUM]
Q: A process shows state Z in ps output. What does that mean?
A) It is sleeping
B) It is a zombie: it has finished but its parent has not collected its exit status
C) It is using zero CPU
D) It is being debugged
ANSWER: B
HINT: The process already terminated, but an entry still exists in the process table.
EXPLAIN: A zombie has exited but its parent has not called wait() to read the exit status, so its process-table entry remains until the parent does (or the parent dies).

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of these commands?
CODE bash
x=1
( x=2; echo "in:$x" )
echo "out:$x"
{ x=3; }
echo "now:$x"
END
A) in:2 out:1 now:3
B) in:2 out:2 now:3
C) in:2 out:1 now:1
D) in:1 out:1 now:3
ANSWER: A
HINT: ( ... ) runs in a subshell (a child process); { ...; } runs in the current shell.
EXPLAIN: Changes inside the subshell are lost when it exits; the brace group changes x in the current shell.

[HARD]
Q: Which command lists the 3 processes using the most memory?
A) ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -4
B) ps aux | sort mem | tail -3
C) top -mem 3
D) free --top 3
ANSWER: A
HINT: A minus sign in --sort means descending. Remember the header line of ps.
EXPLAIN: Sorting by %mem descending and taking 4 lines gives the header plus the top 3 processes.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Shell Scripting

[EASY]
Q: What is the first line of a bash script that tells the system which interpreter to use?
A) #include <bash>
B) #!/bin/bash
C) //bash
D) @bash
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: This line is called the shebang.

[EASY]
Q: In a shell script, what does $1 refer to?
A) The script name
B) The first command-line argument
C) The exit status
D) The number of arguments
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: $0 is the script name, $# is the number of arguments, $? is the last exit status.

[EASY]
Q: Which variable holds the exit status of the last command?
A) $!
B) $?
C) $$
D) $#
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: How do you make script.sh executable?
A) chmod +x script.sh
B) exec script.sh
C) run script.sh
D) chmod 000 script.sh
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which is the correct way to assign a variable in bash?
A) name = "ravi"
B) name="ravi"
C) $name="ravi"
D) set name "ravi"
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: There must be no spaces around = in a bash assignment.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this script?
CODE bash
sum=0
for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do
  if [ $((i % 2)) -eq 0 ]; then
    continue
  fi
  sum=$((sum + i))
done
echo $sum
END
A) 9
B) 15
C) 6
D) 5
ANSWER: A
HINT: continue skips the rest of the loop body for even numbers.
EXPLAIN: Only odd values are added: 1 + 3 + 5 = 9.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this script?
CODE bash
name="Linux"
echo '$name' "$name" ${#name} ${name,,}
END
A) $name Linux 5 linux
B) Linux Linux 5 linux
C) $name Linux 5 LINUX
D) Linux $name 5 linux
ANSWER: A
HINT: Single quotes prevent expansion; ${#var} is the length; ${var,,} converts to lowercase.
EXPLAIN: '$name' is printed literally; "$name" expands to Linux; the length is 5; ${name,,} is linux.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this script?
CODE bash
count() { echo $#; }
count a "b c" d
count "$(echo x y z)"
count $(echo x y z)
END
A) 3 1 3
B) 4 3 3
C) 3 3 3
D) 4 1 3
ANSWER: A
HINT: Quotes keep words together as ONE argument. Unquoted command substitution is split on spaces.
EXPLAIN: "b c" is one argument (3 total); the quoted substitution is one argument; the unquoted one splits into 3.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this script?
CODE bash
f() {
  local x=5
  y=7
  return 3
}
x=1; y=1
f
echo "$? $x $y"
END
A) 3 1 7
B) 0 5 7
C) 3 5 7
D) 3 1 1
ANSWER: A
HINT: local limits a variable to the function. return sets the function's exit status.
EXPLAIN: $? is 3 from return; x was local inside f so the outer x stays 1; y was global, so it becomes 7.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this script?
CODE bash
set -e
false || echo "handled"
echo "after"
false
echo "never"
END
A) handled after
B) handled after never
C) Nothing
D) after never
ANSWER: A
HINT: set -e exits on an unhandled failing command, but not on a command that is part of a || list.
EXPLAIN: The first false is handled by ||, so the script continues; the second false stops the script before "never".

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Networking Commands

[EASY]
Q: Which command checks whether a remote host is reachable by sending ICMP echo requests?
A) ping
B) ssh
C) curl
D) route
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which command shows the IP addresses of the network interfaces on modern Linux?
A) ip addr
B) ipconfig
C) netaddr
D) showip
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: ifconfig is older and may not be installed; ipconfig is the Windows command.

[EASY]
Q: Which command opens a secure remote shell on another machine?
A) telnet
B) ssh
C) ftp
D) rsh
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which command downloads a web page or file from a URL?
A) curl or wget
B) ping
C) nslookup
D) traceroute
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which command looks up the IP address of a domain name using DNS?
A) dig or nslookup
B) ping -d
C) whoami
D) hostnamectl
ANSWER: A

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which command lists all listening TCP ports along with the process using each?
A) ss -tlnp
B) ping -l
C) ip route
D) netstat -r
ANSWER: A
HINT: Look for the options meaning TCP, listening, numeric and process.
EXPLAIN: ss -t (TCP) -l (listening) -n (numeric ports) -p (process); netstat -tlnp is the older equivalent.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which command copies a local file report.pdf to the home directory of user ravi on host 10.0.0.5 over SSH?
A) scp report.pdf ravi@10.0.0.5:~/
B) cp report.pdf ravi@10.0.0.5
C) ssh report.pdf 10.0.0.5
D) ftp report.pdf ravi
ANSWER: A
HINT: The command name is "secure copy", and the remote path is written user@host:path.
EXPLAIN: scp copies files over the SSH protocol; rsync -e ssh is another option.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which command shows each router hop on the path to a destination host?
A) traceroute
B) ping -c 1
C) arp
D) hostname
ANSWER: A
HINT: It works by sending packets with increasing TTL values.
EXPLAIN: Each router that drops a packet whose TTL reached zero replies, revealing one hop at a time.

[HARD]
Q: What does curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" https://example.com print on success?
A) The HTTP status code only, such as 200
B) The full HTML page
C) The response headers
D) Nothing at all
ANSWER: A
HINT: -o sends the body to /dev/null, -s hides the progress bar, -w prints a custom format after the transfer.
EXPLAIN: Only the write-out variable %{http_code} is printed — handy for health-check scripts.

[HARD]
Q: In ip route output, the line "default via 192.168.1.1 dev wlan0" means:
A) All traffic without a more specific route is sent to the gateway 192.168.1.1 through interface wlan0
B) 192.168.1.1 is this computer's own IP address
C) Only traffic to 192.168.1.1 uses wlan0
D) DNS queries go to 192.168.1.1
ANSWER: A
HINT: "default" is the route for 0.0.0.0/0.
EXPLAIN: The default route matches every destination with no more specific route, sending it to the gateway (usually the home router).
